 a singleton pixel layout.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a395ee82/D14/1/code.py:100:    - Mirror the copy vertically when the lattice point is above the origin, mirror it\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a395ee82/D14/1/code.py:101:      horizontally when the point is left of the origin, and apply both mirrors for\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D12/0/code.py:47:#    The generator precomputes every in-bounds 3-pixel L/V placement, then keeps only those whose\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D12/0/code.py:65:#    maroon (9) for lone background landings and hit sprites, blue (1) for multi-projectile\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D12/0/code.py:75:#    - Every non-bg component is still exactly one valid 3-pixel L or V.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D12/0/code.py:198:    - A landing on another sprite recolors that entire sprite to maroon (9); multiple projectiles sharing one empty background cell paint it blue (1); a lone empty-background landing paints maroon (9).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D8/2/code.py:17:        The payload is copied into the other three quadrants: mirror across the horizontal\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D8/2/code.py:18:        axis, mirror across the vertical axis, and point reflection around the same center.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D8/2/code.py:19:        Together with the original quadrant this forms a fourfold symmetric sprite.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D8/2/code.py:25:      Mixed component with a horizontal red line only produced one opposite-side mirror.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D8/2/code.py:52:        would duplicate already mirrored data. The generator enforces one-quadrant input.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D8/2/code.py:100:# Marker counts. Fourfold embedded sprites are larger than the source-task half-mirrors,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D8/2/code.py:237:    # --- 1) Embedded plus-sign markers create three mirrored copies ---\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D8/2/code.py:354:        mirror = np.zeros((H, W), dtype=bool)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D8/2/code.py:366:                mirror[int(rr), int(cc)] = True\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D8/2/code.py:376:        if (payload & mirror).any():\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D8/2/code.py:380:            if payload[int(rr), int(cc)] or mirror[int(rr), int(cc)]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D8/2/code.py:388:            \"mirror\": mirror,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D8/2/code.py:440:            mirror = np.zeros((H, W), dtype=bool)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D8/2/code.py:444:                mirror[r - minr, c - minc] = True\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D8/2/code.py:447:            if payload[red_pos] or mirror[red_pos]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D8/2/code.py:449:            if (payload & mirror).any():\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D8/2/code.py:451:            if _is_4connected_to(payload | mirror, *red_pos):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D8/2/code.py:455:                \"mirror\": mirror,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D8/2/code.py:489:    colored = spr[\"payload\"] | spr[\"mirror\"]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D8/2/code.py:495:def _erase_mirror(canvas: np.ndarray, spr: dict, bg: int) -> None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D8/2/code.py:497:    h, w = spr[\"mirror\"].shape\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D8/2/code.py:498:    canvas[r0 : r0 + h, c0 : c0 + w][spr[\"mirror\"]] = bg\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D8/2/code.py:536:        footprint = spr[\"payload\"] | spr[\"mirror\"]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D8/2/code.py:548:        _erase_mirror(inp, spr, bg)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D12/1/code.py:29:#     Old solver fired up; new solver fires down. Again, the landing cell is mirrored through the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D12/1/code.py:82:# - The solver still assumes every non-bg component meant to matter is a canonical 3-pixel L or V.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D12/1/code.py:246:    - Landing on a sprite recolors the whole hit sprite to maroon (9). Landing on empty background\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D12/1/code.py:247:      paints maroon (9), except that two or more shots sharing the same empty cell paint blue (1).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D12/2/code.py:52:#      maroon(9) means a hit recolor or lone landing, blue(1) means a multi-projectile background collision.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D12/2/code.py:156:    Fire projectiles from 3-pixel triangle sprites; mark collisions with maroon or blue.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D12/2/code.py:159:    - bg = most common color. Non-bg objects are still monochrome 3-pixel L or V triangle sprites, each with a unique geometric apex.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D12/2/code.py:164:    - If the landing cell lies on a sprite, the entire hit sprite is recolored to maroon (9).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D12/2/code.py:165:    - If two or more projectiles land on the same empty background cell, that cell becomes blue (1); a lone empty-cell landing becomes maroon (9).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9385bd28/D11/0/code.py:33:#     - The visible corner markers remain L-shaped 3-pixel anchors placed on one bbox\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9385bd28/D11/0/code.py:158:    - A marker is either a pair of opposite L-shaped corners or a solid 2x2 block of one legend color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D1/1/code.py:23:#    task mirrored the payload across that line. In this variant the red line is no\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D1/1/code.py:31:#      The original task would have mirrored it only to (line-1, c). This is the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D1/1/code.py:63:#    rectangles, so preserving orientation versus mirroring is easy to notice. Having\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9385bd28/D11/1/code.py:20:#   orange, maroon, and grey fake pairs at once. The fill output should still be computed only from\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D4/0/code.py:25:      - horizontal / vertical red line => mirror across that row / column\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D4/0/code.py:68:    - output-first generation places full output groups first, then erases mirrored copies to derive the input\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D4/0/code.py:70:    - mirrored copies are checked to stay in-bounds by construction because the whole output footprint is placed as a unit\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D4/0/code.py:73:    - red-line groups are generated so point reflection is visibly different from old line-axis mirroring\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D4/0/code.py:314:            new_mirror = {(-int(r), int(c)) for r, c in shape}\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D4/0/code.py:315:            old_mirror = {(-int(r), -int(c)) for r, c in shape}\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D4/0/code.py:328:            new_mirror = {(int(r), -int(c)) for r, c in shape}\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D4/0/code.py:329:            old_mirror = {(-int(r), -int(c)) for r, c in shape}\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D4/0/code.py:331:        if new_mirror == old_mirror:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D4/0/code.py:339:        for cell in new_mirror:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D4/0/code.py:364:            new_mirror = {(-int(r), int(line_len - 1 - c)) for r, c in shape}\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D4/0/code.py:365:            old_mirror = {(-int(r), int(c)) for r, c in shape}\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D