d place enlarged tiled sprites on the canvas using a legend that now encodes both rotation and mirroring.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a32d8b75/D5/1/code.py:257:    Key insight: The marker panel is now a two-channel instruction: endpoint geometry controls rotation, and the center color controls whether the rotated composite is mirrored.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/abc82100/D4/2/code.py:27:#   stencil is mirrored across that forward axis.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/abc82100/D4/2/code.py:34:#   if the side arm is on the south side, that rotated stencil is mirrored before\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/abc82100/D4/2/code.py:40:# - Each rule gets multiple scattered seeds, and every rule uses both mirror states\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/abc82100/D4/2/code.py:65:# - The input now contains repeated L-shaped local cues around the source centers, so\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/abc82100/D4/2/code.py:251:    Use local source-target-pattern examples to stamp rotated or mirrored copies.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/abc82100/D4/2/code.py:266:      arm is on the opposite side, mirror the rotated stencil before stamping.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7ed72f31/D4/1/code.py:35:  a red line inside a mixed sprite means mirror across that line; one red pixel\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D4/1/code.py:34:#    color 9, because a hit on an already-maroon sprite would hide the local recolor.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D4/1/code.py:51:#    requires at least one hit event, keeps the target sprite non-maroon, and caps hit count at\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D4/1/code.py:52:#    2 so the usual case is one or two maroon pixels inside otherwise unchanged 3-pixel sprites.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D4/1/code.py:53:#    This makes the difference from \"entire sprite turns maroon\" visible across a few examples.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D4/1/code.py:124:    Fire pro